LOK: find Visual Studio 2026, which no longer registers the old component

The build looks for the compiler with vswhere and asks for
Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.x86.x64. Visual Studio 2026
does not register that id, so every query comes back empty and the build
stops with "no Visual Studio 2026 installation found" -- even though the
compiler is there.

When the year is 2026, ask for the product instead and let the checks
that follow (the compiler, the Windows SDK) decide whether the
installation is usable.
diff --git a/configure.ac b/configure.ac
index 9f67ee4..902ff76 100644
--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -4515,6 +4515,13 @@ find_msvc()
         if test -z "$vswhereoutput"; then
             vswhereoutput=`$vswhere -prerelease -version "@<:@ $ver , $(expr ${ver%%.*} + 1) @:}@" -requires Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.x86.x64 -property installationPath | head -1`
         fi
+        # Visual Studio 2026 does not register the old component id, so the
+        # queries above find nothing.  Ask for the product itself; the checks
+        # that follow look for the compiler and the Windows SDK, and they
+        # decide whether the installation is usable.
+        if test -z "$vswhereoutput" && test "$ver" = "18.0"; then
+            vswhereoutput=`$vswhere -products \* -version "@<:@ $ver , $(expr ${ver%%.*} + 1) @:}@" -property installationPath | head -1`
+        fi
         # Fall back to all MS products (this includes VC++ Build Tools)
         if ! test -n "$vswhereoutput"; then
             AC_MSG_CHECKING([VC++ Build Tools and similar])